          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#ProblemsWithTheConcept"} Problems with the concept
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#DependenceOnRenormalizationScheme"} 1) Dependence on renormalization scheme
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#2_general_viability"} 2) General viability
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle+physics"}> particle
            \-)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            the term “naturalness” &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#tHooft1980"}> ‘t Hooft 1980
            ,
            = succeed "&rbrack;" do
              %a{:href => "#GellMann83"} Gell- Mann 1983
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28physics%29"} model
            of physics is expected to work without requiring unlikely-looking ad-hoc coincidences or “fine-tuning” of its parameters (such as the choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            constants), and that instead all such “conspiracies” among parameter values are to have some systematic cause, such as some (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/broken+symmetry"}> broken
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry"} symmetry
            of the model.
          %p
            An archetypical example, which at times has been referred to as
            %em the
            naturalness probem of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} mass
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+boson"} Higgs boson
            is first of all many orders of magnitude below the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck+scale"} Planck scale
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} quantum corrections
            from the presence of (potentially undetected) heavy particles, which therefore must coincidentally cancel out. This is also called the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hierarchy+problem"} hierarchy problem
          %p
            Accordingly, a popular suggestion has been that there should be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry"} symmetry
            in nature which is to naturally explain this otherwise coincidental cancellation, namely low-energy
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LHC"} LHC
            experiment is leading the high energy physics community to a re-examination of the naturalness paradigm and/or to focus on alternative mechanisms, such as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composite+Higgs+boson"} composite Higgs boson
          %p
            The next main example of (lack of) naturalness often considered is the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+energy"}> dark energy
            ), which seems tiny when compared to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} quantum corrections
            which it naively receives from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+energy"} vacuum energy
            of all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            in the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable universe
          %h2#ProblemsWithTheConcept Problems with the concept
          %p
            A key problem is arguably that the principle of naturalness has been used in a very vague and very ambiguous sense. Even apart from the problem of which large or small numbers are to be regarded as “unlikely” (see the
            %a{:href => "#WilsonOnNaturalness"} quote below
            from
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Wilson04"} Wilson 04
          %h3#DependenceOnRenormalizationScheme 1) Dependence on renormalization scheme
          %p
            Both the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} mass
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+particle"} Higgs particle
            as well as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            are subject to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
          %p
            (For more on the
            = succeed "-freedom" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            see
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "cosmological+constant#InPerturbativeQuantumGravity"} there
          %p
            A key fact, that seems to be mostly overlooked in discussion of naturalness/fine-tuning, is that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            parameters a priori take values in an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+space"} affine space
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "geometry+of+physics+--+perturbative+quantum+field+theory#ExistenceRenormalization"} this theorem
          %p
            This means that without choosing an arbitrary origin and then a set of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinates"} coordinates
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} quantum correction
            is “large” or “small”. An origin and a set of coordinates is however provided by a choice of
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization+scheme"} renormalization scheme
          %h3#2_general_viability 2) General viability
          %p
            Even if one assumes that the parameters under consideration are invariantly defined
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/numbers"} numbers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization+schemes"} renormalization schemes
            (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#DependenceOnRenormalizationScheme"} above
          %p#FromKane17
            The following is from
            %a{:href => "#Kane17"} Kane 17
            (there in a discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%E2%82%82-MSSM"} G₂-MSSM
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28physics%29"} model building
          %blockquote
            %p
              Until recently there were no theories predicting the values of superpartner masses. The arguments based on ‘naturalness’ are basically like saying the weather tomorrow should be the same as today. The opposite of naturalness is having a theory.
              = succeed "\u2026" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              Claims
              = succeed "superpartners" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              should have been seen would be valid given so called naturalness arguments, but are wrong in actual theories. Many of us think that is a misuse of the idea of naturalness, but it is the fashionable use.
          %p (p. 33 (3-2))
          %blockquote
            %p
              Some arguments (‘naturalness’) can be used to estimate what values
              = succeed "MSSM" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              might have. If those arguments were correct some superpartners would already have been discovered at the CERN LHC. It would have been nice if the naturalness arguments had worked, but they did not. Since they were not predictions from a theory it is not clear how to interpret that.
          %p (p. 39 (4-3))
          %blockquote
            %p The failure of naïve naturalness to describe the world tells us we should look harder for a theory that does, an ‘ultraviolet completion’. Compactified string/ M-theories appear to be strong candidates for such a theory.
            %p#TheAlternative The alternative to naturalness, often neglected as an alternative, is having a theory.
          %p (p. 57 (6-1))
          %p
            Similarly
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Wilson04"} Wilson 04, p. 10
          %blockquote
            %p#WilsonOnNaturalness
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [ \cdots ]
              The claim was that it would be unnatural for such particles to have masses small enough to be detectable soon. But this claim makes no sense when one becomes familiar with the history of physics. There have been a number of cases where numbers arose that were unexpectedly small or large. An early example was the very large distance to the nearest star as compared to the distance to the Sun, as needed by Copernicus, because otherwise the nearest stars would have exhibited measurable parallax as the Earth moved around the Sun. Within elementary particle physics, one has unexpectedly large ratios of masses, such as the large ratio of the muon mass to the electron mass. There is also the very small value of the weak coupling constant. In the time since my paper was written, another set of unexpectedly small masses was discovered: the neutrino masses. There is also the riddle of dark energy in cosmology, with its implication of possibly an extremely small value for the cosmological constant in Einstein’s theory of general relativity. This blunder was potentially more serious, if it caused any subsequent researchers to dismiss possibilities for very large or very small values for parameters that now must be taken seriously.
            %p
              (
              %a{:href => "#Wilson04"}> Wilson 04, p. 10
              )
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hierarchy+problem"} hierarchy problem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+supersymmetry"} split supersymmetry
          %h2#references References
          %p Early discussion:
          %ul
            %li#tHooft1980
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gerard+%27t+Hooft"}> Gerard 't Hooft
                ,
                %em Naturalness, Chiral Symmetry, and Spontaneous Chiral Symmetry Breaking
                In: G. ‘Hooft et al.,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Recent Developments in Gauge Theories
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 59
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4684-7571-5_9"} doi:10.1007/978-1-4684-7571-5_9
              %blockquote
                %p
                  \&amp;lbrack;pp 135:&amp;rbrack; “it is unlikely that the microscopic equations contain various free parameters that are carefully adjusted by Nature to give cancelling effects such that the macroscopic systems have some special properties. This is a philosophy which we would like to apply to the unified gauge theories: the effective interactions at a large length scale, corresponding to a low energy scale
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi μ
                          %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_1
                  should follow from the properties at a much smaller length scale, or higher energy scale
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
